1	/******************************************************************************
2	 * Top contributors (to current version):
3	 *   Mudathir Mohamed
4	 *
5	 * This file is part of the cvc5 project.
6	 *
7	 * Copyright (c) 2009-2026 by the authors listed in the file AUTHORS
8	 * in the top-level source directory and their institutional affiliations.
9	 * All rights reserved.  See the file COPYING in the top-level source
10	 * directory for licensing information.
11	 * ****************************************************************************
12	 * Extension to the theory of arithmetic handling the lia* (LIA star) operator.
13	 *
14	 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
15	 * What the lia* operator means
16	 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
17	 * A literal
18	 *     (int.star-contains (lambda ((x_1 Int) ... (x_n Int)) p) v_1 ... v_n)
19	 * asserts that the vector v = (v_1, ..., v_n) belongs to the *star* (additive
20	 * closure) of the set
21	 *     S = { x in Z^n : p(x) and x >= 0 }.
22	 * The star of S is
23	 *     S* = { the sum of finitely many (>= 0) elements of S },
24	 * with the empty sum giving the zero vector. So v in S* iff v can be written as
25	 * a finite sum of vectors each of which satisfies the predicate p (and is
26	 * non-negative). This is the integer analogue of Kleene star in the monoid
27	 * (Z^n, +); the sets S* are exactly the (non-negative) semilinear sets.
28	 *
29	 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
30	 * The decision procedure (Levatich, Bjorner, Piskac, Shoham, VMCAI 2020,
31	 * "Solving LIA* Using Approximations")
32	 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
33	 * 1. Put p into disjunctive normal form, p <=> D_1 or ... or D_m, where each
34	 *    D_j is a conjunction of linear (in)equalities, i.e. a convex polyhedron.
35	 *    Then S = S_1 union ... union S_m, with S_j the integer points of D_j (in
36	 *    the non-negative orthant). (See liastar_utils.cpp for the normalization.)
37	 *
38	 * 2. The additive closure distributes over the union as a Minkowski sum:
39	 *        S* = S_1* + ... + S_m*,
40	 *    because any sum of elements of S can be grouped by the polyhedron each
41	 *    summand came from. So a vector is in S* iff it is the sum of one
42	 *    contribution from each cone's semigroup S_j* (each including 0).
43	 *
44	 * 3. For each polyhedron Normaliz computes a Hilbert basis and module
45	 *    generators. The integer points of D_j are
46	 *        { g + sum_k l_k h_k : g a module generator, l_k >= 0 },
47	 *    where the h_k are the Hilbert basis of the recession cone (its rays). The
48	 *    semigroup S_j* is then captured by giving each module generator g a
49	 *    non-negative multiplier mu_g (how many copies of g are taken) and each ray
50	 *    a multiplier l_k, coupled by `mu_g = 0 => l_k = 0` (a ray of g is only
51	 *    available when g itself is used). See `LiaStarUtils::getGeneratorBody`.
52	 *
53	 * 4. Summing these contributions over all cones gives the *star constraints*: a
54	 *    system of linear constraints over fresh non-negative integers whose
55	 *    satisfiability is equivalent to `v in S*`. The extension then emits the
56	 *    reduction lemma `literal = star`.
57	 *
58	 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
59	 * Two strategies
60	 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
61	 * Eager (`eagerCheckStar`): compute the full DNF, build every cone up front and
62	 *   emit the exact equivalence `literal = star` in one shot.
63	 *
64	 * Lazy (`lazyCheckStar` / `lazyHilbert`): the DNF can blow up, so instead keep a
65	 *   persistent incremental QF_LIA subsolver seeded with p (and x >= 0). Each
66	 *   round it produces a model in a region of p not yet covered by a cone; the
67	 *   convex cell containing that model is read off (`LiaStarUtils::getDisjunct`),
68	 *   turned into a cone, and added to the accumulated set. The star built from
69	 *   the cones found so far is an *under-approximation*, so the equivalence is
70	 *   asserted only tentatively under a fresh boolean guard `g` (`g => (literal =
71	 *   star)`), biased true, until the subsolver becomes unsat -- meaning every
72	 *   cell of p is covered and the encoding is exact, at which point the
73	 *   unconditional equivalence is emitted.
74	 *
75	 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
76	 * Where lemmas come from
77	 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
78	 * `checkFullEffort` is the entry point. For each STAR_CONTAINS assertion it
79	 * emits:
80	 *   - a non-negativity lemma (the vector lives in the non-negative orthant);
81	 *   - a split on the substituted predicate `p[v]` (if v itself satisfies p it
82	 *     is a single-summand member of S*);
83	 *   - and then, unless the current model already satisfies the literal, the
84	 *     star reduction via the eager or lazy strategy.
85	 */
